<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kololo Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1840 CE – 1864 CE (approx.) · capital Linyanti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sotho conquest state on the upper Zambezi, visited by Livingstone, whose language still shapes Lozi speech.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebetwane">Sebetwane</a> led the Kololo, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Sotho</a> group displaced in the 1820s, on a long migration across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>. Around 1840 they conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> floodplain, setting up their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linyanti">Linyanti</a> in the Chobe swamps.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebetwane">Sebetwane</a> ruled with skill, incorporating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> and other subjects and spreading his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Sotho</a> language, which became the basis of modern Silozi. The Kololo lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, Lozi labour and tribute in grain, fish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> reached Sebetwane in 1851 and later travelled to the Atlantic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coasts with Kololo porters provided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekeletu">Sekeletu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebetwane">Sebetwane</a> died in 1851; under his sickly son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekeletu">Sekeletu</a>, malaria thinned the Kololo and loyalty collapsed. After Sekeletu's death in 1863 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> rising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipopa">Sipopa</a> killed most Kololo men in 1864, restoring the Lozi monarchy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1840 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.32%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1840 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Linyanti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sebetwane, Mamochisane, Sekeletu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sesotho (Sekololo), Silozi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>, <a href="/countries/namibia">Namibia</a>, <a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Makololo, Kololo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=kololo-kingdom" aria-label="Kololo Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5328 2856 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kololo Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5648 3112L5629 3000"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5629 3000h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5648 3112h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5648 3112h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Linyanti. <a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=kololo-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
